all three-day heatwave sounds lovely  doesn't it for most of us at least  temperatures should reach more than 30  celsius  that means a busy weekend on britain's  beaches and with  beaches with a water comes some danger  let's talk to ross mcleod from the royal  national lifeboat institute who's there  in poole bay  endorsement for us this morning it's  good to see you look i mean i i know the  rnli are not killjoys  in any way shape or form but you've you  know we need to understand the dangers  of beaches and dangers of the water  don't we  yeah absolutely good good morning  everyone and yeah we're down here today  in paul bay a beautiful day as you can  see and  everyone's had a really tough few months  um and it's no surprise that with jan  elia and our colleagues at the coast  guard are expecting it to be  one of the busiest summers on record and  with this good weather no doubt many  thousands of people will flock to the  coast and who can blame them  and so what we're really calling for  people to do is just to make sure have a  little bit of thought around  what potentially could go wrong  obviously we want everyone to have a  fantastic time at the coast  make some amazing memories and go home  safe at the end of the day so there's  just a few  things that you can do to make sure you  keep yourself and your family safe and  also  know what to do should the worst happen  okay so so what are some of these things  because i know the rnli has run  a campaign for a few years now just  talking about  respecting the water  exactly it is around just having a  healthy respect for the water and  start breaking that down the first thing  you can do is try and go to a supervised  place so especially if you're heading to  the coast  try and find a life-guarded beach and  the rnli runs around 245 beaches across  the uk so  there's there's beaches all over the  country plenty to choose from and when  you're there at these beaches we'd  encourage you to swim between the red  and yellow flags and that way you know  you've got a trained professional on  hand and  these guys and girls did a brilliant job  every year and last year  we know they saved around 110 lives so  if you are in these locations if  something does go wrong  you've got help close at hand and also  if you're going to any beach whether  it's lifeguarded or not if you find  yourself in difficulty in the water  remember the technique float to live and  this is just simply rest and relaxing in  the water extending your arms and legs  trying to float a bit like a starfish  put a bit of treading water if you need  to but the main thing is not to panic  whether you're getting swept out in a  rip current or you're falling in  unexpectedly  the worst thing you can do is panic so  just chill out in the water  uh wait for help to arrive or gather  your thoughts and your breath  um and call for help yourself and the  final one if you see anybody else in  trouble  is to remember to call 999 and ask for  the coast guard and that way  again we can get emergency services to  you no doubt it's going to be a busy  weekend  for everyone across the blue light  community but we really want to make  sure people put those calls in early  and so we can get to you and help and  try and resist the urge to go in the  water yourself because unfortunately  every year we see too many tragic  examples of the havago heroes that  unfortunately lose their lives as well  so make that call early um if you want  to try and help  find something that floats like a rescue  ring or a throw line or even an  inflatable football throw that into the  water  encourage them to calm down encourage  them to try and float but please don't  cut yourself  in danger as well i mean the the weather  might be gloriously hot this weekend but  we  that doesn't necessarily mean the water  is going to be going to be particularly  warm does it  no i mean we're expecting 25 to 30  degree air temperature but the water is  unlikely to reach above  13 or 14 degrees in most places and  probably a lot colder in land  and this can trigger something called  cold water shock if you jump in and it's  a  physiological reaction when your skin  temperature changes rapidly  we've done a lot of work with this with  professor tipton at the university of  portsmouth  and if you even if you're a good swimmer  if you jump into cold water this can  cause a spike in your heart rate it can  lose you cause you to lose control of  your breathing  and even in strong swimmers this can be  really dangerous quite quickly so we'd  encourage you to  enter the water slowly um if you're in  any doubt if you're not in the water  often  there's no shame in wearing a wetsuit  and this will also help keep you afloat  as well so  just take it easy acclimatise to the  water have a great and fantastic time  this this weekend but  just have half an eye on safety and make  sure you know what to do in an emergency  ross i hope you have a very quiet  weekend  fingers crossed thanks for talking to us  thank you
 

